<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anselm of Canterbury</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1033–1109</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anselm of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> (c. 1033–1109) was an Italian-born monk, abbot of Bec in Normandy and from 1093 Archbishop of Canterbury. A leading theologian of early scholasticism, he formulated the ontological argument for the existence of God and wrote Cur Deus Homo on the atonement. As archbishop he quarrelled with William II and Henry I over royal control of the Church.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anselm-of-canterbury.jpg" alt="Anselm of Canterbury" width="320" height="382" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anselm_of_Canterbury,_seal.jpg: The original uploader was Srnec at English Wikipedia. derivative work: MLWatts · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anselm_of_Canterbury,_seal.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
